@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+# TXT narration with audio and SRT together
+
+In **Voice cloning**, import a TXT file using **Import script**. This replaces
+rather than appends to the current script, remembers the TXT basename, and enables
+**Save audio + SRT (one subtitle per script line)**. A manually entered script
+can also enable the checkbox; without an imported filename it uses the usual take
+name.
+
+Each non-empty physical line is rendered independently, using the selected voice,
+language, quality and delivery settings. Soft wrapping in the editor does not
+create subtitle lines. SRT keeps the original text, including Japanese and Korean;
+normalization and pronunciation substitutions affect the spoken version only.
+Blank lines are ignored. Automatic duration is required in this mode.
+
+The final WAV is assembled in order without crossfading across TXT lines. Every
+cue covers the corresponding generated segment, measured in audio samples, so no
+transcription pass or ASR model is needed. Cues include any silence the speech
+model produces within that segment; they are segment boundaries, not word-level
+forced alignment. Generating separate sentences may change rhythm compared with
+rendering a whole paragraph. Generated speech should still be listened to for
+omissions, repetitions or pronunciation mistakes.
+
+After synthesis, the existing output **Save audio + SRT** button opens one native
+save dialog. Choosing `Bai_01.wav` writes both `Bai_01.wav` and `Bai_01.srt` in that
+directory. The adjacent format menu also offers MP3 with the same SRT. UTF-8 with
+a BOM preserves multilingual subtitle text in Windows editors. MP3 is encoded
+from the same WAV timeline; encoder padding may be handled differently by older
+players. Neither file is overwritten; choose another name or directory if either
+already exists. A failed write rolls back new files, and a failed subtitle
+download never writes the audio half.
+
+## Multiple TXT files
+
+Select several TXT files in **Import script**. Only TXT is accepted for a
+multi-file import. The queue lists them in natural filename order (`Bai_2` before
+`Bai_10`). The editor shows the first file; edits there affect that first file.
+Choose **WAV + SRT** or **MP3 + SRT**, then use the usual **Synthesize audio** action.
+Choose the destination directory once. Each file is synthesized and its pair is
+saved before the next starts, using that TXT's basename. The composer reports the
+current filename and file index. The voice, language and generation settings are
+snapshotted for the entire queue. Use separate batches for different languages or
+voices.
+
+Cancel stops the remaining queue. Complete pairs already saved remain; a pair
+being written finishes its save, and disk/backend errors stop subsequent files.
+The batch folder authorization is revoked at completion, failure or cancellation.
+**Keep only the first file** returns to single-file operation. The queue is kept
+only for the current app session.
+
+## Existing recordings and models
+
+Recordings made before line timing was enabled do not gain an SRT automatically.
+Re-render their source TXT with the checkbox enabled, or transcribe the existing
+recording. The custom feature does not change any model's license or voice consent
+requirements. Existing downloaded engines, profiles and projects remain in their
+usual storage locations.
+
+## Validation
+
+Focused renderer/main tests cover request flags, source filenames, ordered batch
+render/save, a single directory selection, MP3 routes, failed saves, duplicate
+outputs and capability revocation. Model-free backend tests cover sample-based
+cue timing, Unicode, rate changes, final audio length, and publication/rollback of
+the WAV/SRT pair. `electron/tests/txt-srt-smoke.mjs` exercises the Vietnamese clone
+UI in Chromium with a mocked backend and native save bridge. These checks do not
+certify speech quality or GPU inference on a Windows device.
